---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { helpers } from '../../lib/helpers';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers(lang);
const P = h.P;
const U = P.us;
const s = (k: Parameters<typeof h.src>[0], t: string) => h.src(k, t);
---
<PageLayout title="Citizenship test method: questions, pass rules and dates" description="How Citizenship Test Free draws a mock interview from the USCIS lists, applies the officer's stop rule, finds the first N-400 filing date and adds up the fees." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the questions, pass rules and dates are built', description: 'Method of Citizenship Test Free', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="kicker">Method</p><h1 class="mt-0 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">How the questions, pass rules and dates are built</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <p>Every page and every tool reads the same files: the two official USCIS question lists, the USCIS reading and writing vocabulary, and one parameter file of official rules and fees, read on uscis.gov on {h.date(U.retrieved_at)}. No pass mark, fee or day limit is typed into a page; when a rule changes, the parameter file changes and every page follows.</p>
    <p><strong>Questions.</strong> The banks hold the {h.us.n25} civics questions of 2025 (<Fragment set:html={s('us_128', 'USCIS form M-1778')} />) and the {h.us.n08} of 2008, with every accepted answer, exactly as USCIS publishes them: they are works of the federal government, in the public domain. Answers that depend on who holds an office come from the <Fragment set:html={s('us_updates', 'USCIS test updates page')} />, reviewed by USCIS on {h.date(h.us.updatesReviewed)}; the questions whose answer depends on where you live are shown in the lists but never scored. The four choices of each practice question are ours, and an automated test checks on every build that the right choice matches an accepted USCIS answer and that the four choices differ.</p>
    <p><strong>Marking.</strong> A 2025 mock interview draws up to {U.test2025.asked} scorable questions at random with a seed, passes at {U.test2025.passCorrect} right and stops, as the officer does, at {U.test2025.passCorrect} right or {U.test2025.failWrong} wrong. The 2008 test uses {U.test2008.asked}, {U.test2008.passCorrect} and {U.test2008.failWrong}; the 65/20 test draws {U.s6520.asked} of the 20 starred questions, {U.s6520.passCorrect} to pass. These rules come from the <Fragment set:html={s('us_study', 'USCIS study page')} /> and the <Fragment set:html={s('us_pm_e2', 'USCIS Policy Manual')} />.</p>
    <p><strong>N-400 date.</strong> The tool tries every day from today and stops at the first one on which all of these hold: early filing ({U.n400.earlyFilingDays} days before the green card anniversary), physical presence ({h.num(U.n400.routes['5'].presenceDays)} days in 5 years or {h.num(U.n400.routes['3'].presenceDays)} in 3), no trip of one year or more in the period (or a wait of {U.n400.afterBreakWait['5']} after the return), three years of marriage on the spouse route and age {U.n400.minAge}. Departure and return days count as days present (<Fragment set:html={s('us_pm_d4', 'Policy Manual 12 D.4')} />). A trip of more than {U.n400.absencePresumptionDays} days is flagged because USCIS presumes a break in residence, which can be rebutted (<Fragment set:html={s('us_pm_d3', '12 D.3')} />). The USCIS worked example (green card on 4 July 2017, filing from 5 April 2022) is one of our automated tests.</p>
    <p><strong>Fees.</strong> {h.usd(U.fees.online)} online, {h.usd(U.fees.paper)} on paper and {h.usd(U.fees.reduced)} reduced, from the <Fragment set:html={s('us_n400', 'N-400 page')} />; income limits from the <Fragment set:html={s('us_fpg', 'USCIS poverty guidelines table')} /> in force from {h.date(U.fpg.effective)}. Processing times: the national median per fiscal year comes from the <Fragment set:html={s('us_hist_pt', 'USCIS historical processing times fact sheet')} /> (data as of {h.date(U.processing.dataAsOf)}); times per field office are only in the USCIS online tool, which does not allow automated reading, so the reader types the figure shown there. Steps without a published duration (receipt notice, ceremony) are milestones on the timelines; the 120-day decision rule and the 30 or 33 days for a hearing request come from the <Fragment set:html={s('us_pm_b4', 'Policy Manual')} />.</p>
    <h2>Checks</h2>
    <p>Automated tests run on every build: every question has an accepted answer among its four choices and four distinct choices; every mock interview has the right length and stops at the right count; the USCIS worked example for the early filing date gives the official date; trips are counted in whole days abroad, the day of departure and the day of return being days present; the fees add up. A script re-reads the N-400 fees on uscis.gov every month and stops the update if an amount differs from ours, until a person has checked it.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It does not judge good moral character, tax filing, Selective Service registration or any criminal or immigration history: only the residence rules and the tests.</li>
      <li>It does not reproduce the reading and writing sentences the officer will use, which USCIS does not publish: our practice sentences are built from the official word lists.</li>
      <li>It does not schedule an interview or send an application: that is done only on uscis.gov or by mail to USCIS.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Method version: {LAST_UPDATED}. A figure that looks wrong: <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact</a>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
